1... Nf6 2. Bf4 g6 ( 2... e6 3. e3 d5 4. Nd2 ) ( 2... d5 { Transposes to 1...d5 2.Bf4 Nf6. } ) 3. e3 Bg7 ( 3... d6 { Transposes to 1...Nf6 2.Bf4 d6 3.e3 g6. } ) 4. Nf3 O-O ( 4... d6 5. h3 { Gives the bishop h2 before ...Nh5 or ...e5 can chase it. } 5... O-O ( 5... c5 6. c3 ) ( 5... Nbd7 6. Be2 ) 6. Be2 { Transposes to the main line, 4...O-O 5.Be2 d6 6.h3. } ) ( 4... d5 5. Be2 O-O 6. O-O c5 7. c3 { Backs up d4. Next h3 and Nbd2, with the same quiet set-up as against ...d6. } ) 5. Be2 { Against a fianchetto the bishop belongs on e2: it covers the kingside and leaves d3 for a pawn if needed. } 5... d6 ( 5... d5 { Transposes to 4...d5 5.Be2 O-O. } ) 6. h3 { Gives the bishop on f4 the retreat square h2, so ...Nh5 or ...e5 cannot trap it. } 6... Nbd7 ( 6... c5 7. c3 ) ( 6... Nc6 7. O-O ) ( 6... Re8 7. O-O ) 7. O-O b6 ( 7... Re8 8. Bh2 ) 8. Bh2 { Out of the way of ...e5 and ...Nh5, and it still watches the long diagonal toward Black's king. } 8... Bb7 9. a4 { Stops ...b5 and gains space on the queenside. } 9... a6 10. c3 { Supports d4 and makes room for Nbd2 and Qc2. With the centre solid, Black has no way to open it favourably. Plan: Nbd2, Qc2 and e4. } *
